  "step_3": "The decision reason requires consideration of variance and a preference for the option with higher variance. While Lottery A has significantly higher variance, the individual does not reference any concept related to variance or distributional spread. They note equal expected loss (same expected value), which is relevant to other decision rules (e.g., expected value indifference), but not variance. The justification is based on an emotional desire to take risk versus a rational choice of the 'lesser evil,' which likely corresponds to the smaller loss with higher probability (Lottery B). This suggests the individual may ultimately favor B, which contradicts the decision reason’s preference for A. Even if the individual were drawn to risk, the absence of any structural consideration of variance or variability in outcomes indicates the reason was not used in deliberation. The conflict is framed affectively (gut vs. head), not analytically. Therefore, there is no evidence the individual computed or considered variance as a basis for choice.",
  "final_assessment_score": 10
